<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chersonesus Taurica</h1><p class="st-meta">422 BCE – 110 BCE (approx.) · capital Chersonesus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Democratic Greek city in south-western Crimea, colony of Heraclea Pontica, whose ruins near Sevastopol preserve a whole ancient landscape of farm plots.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/heraclea-pontica">Heraclea Pontica</a>, joined according to tradition by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>, founded Chersonesus in 422-421 BCE on a small bay in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, perhaps on the site of an earlier trading post. Over the 4th century BCE the city took control of the Heracleian peninsula and parts of the north-west Crimean coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chersonesus was a democracy whose citizens swore an oath, preserved in a 3rd-century BCE inscription, to defend the city and never betray it. Its farmland was divided into regular rectangular plots, many planted with vineyards, and wine, fish and grain were its main exports. Its patron deity was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenos-chersonesus">Maiden</a> (Parthenos).</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scilurus">Scilurus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palakus">Palacus</a>, the city appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> c. 110 BCE; his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diophantus-general">Diophantus</a> defended it, and it passed under Pontic and later Roman protection. As Byzantine Cherson it survived into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, where tradition places the baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir of Kiev</a> in 988.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chersonesus-taurica.jpg" alt="Chersonesus Taurica" width="320" height="181"><figcaption>Image: Dmitry A. Mottl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chersonesos_ruins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>422 BCE – 110 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chersonesus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chersonesos, Cherson, Chersonesus, Korsun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=chersonesus-taurica" aria-label="Chersonesus Taurica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5480 600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chersonesus Taurica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5800 856L5879 982M5800 856L5866 834M5800 856L5304 942M5800 856L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5879 982h0M5866 834h0M5304 942h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5800 856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5800 856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=chersonesus-taurica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

